"comparison": "Both JPMorgan and Bank of America disclose the specific impact of loan modifications on loan metrics, particularly term extensions. JPMorgan reports that financial distress modifications (FDMs) in residential real estate extended the weighted-average loan life by 15 years, with modifications predominantly consisting of term extensions and interest rate reductions. Similarly, Bank of America discloses that term extensions granted to troubled borrowers increased the weighted-average life of impacted loans by 1.7 years in 2024. While both track the extension of loan life as a primary metric of modification concessions, JPMorgan's disclosed extension period (15 years) is significantly longer than Bank of America's (1.7 years), and JPMorgan provides specific details on interest rate reductions (from 7.53% to 5.44%) which are not quantified in the comparable Bank of America text.",
